-- ─── CUSTOMER (12 situations, 4 premium) ──────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO situations (slug, category, title, situation_body, biblical_principle, scripture_refs, practical_response, prayer, reflection_question, is_premium)
VALUES

-- Customer 1
('customer-lies-about-credit',
 'customer',
 'The Customer Who Lies About Their Credit',
 'You are sitting at the desk with a customer who insists their credit is "great" — they have told you this three times. The credit application comes back at 520. Now they are embarrassed and defensive, and the manager is pushing you to "work with what we have" — which means a payment you know they cannot afford long-term.',
 'Truthfulness serves the customer''s long-term wellbeing more than closing a deal today. Proverbs reminds us that honest words endure, while deception collapses under its own weight.',
 '[{"reference": "Proverbs 12:19", "text": "Truthful lips endure forever, but a lying tongue is but for a moment."}]',
 'Name what you see without shaming them. Say something like: "The numbers came back lower than we both expected. Let me show you what is realistic so you can make a decision that works for you six months from now, not just today." If your manager insists on a deal structure you believe is predatory, ask a direct question: "Would you put your own family member in this payment?"',
 'Lord, give me the courage to tell the truth even when it costs me a deal. Help me see this customer as someone you love — someone with a family, bills, and a future that outlasts this transaction. Let me be a person they can trust, even if they do not buy from me today.',
 'When have you been tempted to let a customer believe something about their financial situation that you knew was not true? What made it hard to be honest?',
 false),

-- Customer 2 (premium)
('customer-haggle-endlessly',
 'customer',
 'Endless Haggling That Wears You Down',
 'A customer has been on your lot for three hours. They have negotiated every line item. They have called their brother-in-law twice for advice. They want a discount on a car already priced below market. Your manager will not approve another dollar. They are still at your desk, and fresh ups are walking through the door.',
 'Patience is not a strategy for getting what you want — it is the shape of love in a transaction. Paul describes love as patient and kind, not irritable. This customer may be anxious, not malicious. Their life may feel out of control, and this negotiation is the one place they feel they have power.',
 '[{"reference": "1 Corinthians 13:4", "text": "Love is patient and kind; love does not envy or boast; it is not arrogant."}]',
 'Set a gentle boundary: "I have given you every discount I can. Here is my best number. If you need time to think about it, I am here when you are ready — even if that is next week." Then let them go. Your peace is worth more than the gross on this deal.',
 'Father, when my patience runs out, remind me that I am not just selling a car — I am interacting with a person you made. Give me the grace to stay kind even when the deal is not going anywhere.',
 'What is underneath your impatience with difficult customers? Is it the lost time, the lost commission, or something else?',
 true),

-- Customer 3
('customer-takes-credit-for-referral',
 'customer',
 'The Customer Who Claims a Referral That Was Never Made',
 'A customer insists they were referred by a previous buyer of yours, asking for a discount accordingly. You check the name. There is no record. When you gently ask for the referrer''s contact info, they get flustered and defensive.',
 'Integrity does not require you to publicly expose someone''s small deception, but it does require you not to participate in it. Jesus told his followers to be "wise as serpents and innocent as doves" — shrewd enough to see what is happening, pure enough not to retaliate.',
 '[{"reference": "Matthew 10:16", "text": "Behold, I am sending you out as sheep in the midst of wolves, so be wise as serpents and innocent as doves."}]',
 'Treat them with dignity. Say: "I want to help you get the best deal I can offer, whether there was a referral or not. Let me see what I can do." You preserve the relationship without endorsing the dishonesty.',
 'Lord, help me to be discerning without becoming cynical. Let me see people clearly without looking down on them.',
 'Have you ever been tempted to embellish a deal to make it sound better to a customer? What stopped you — or what did not?',
 false),

-- Customer 4 (premium)
('customer-racist-comments',
 'customer',
 'A Customer Makes a Racist Comment on the Floor',
 'You are walking a customer to a vehicle and they make a remark about a coworker, another customer, or people in the neighborhood that is clearly racist. It is casual, as if they assume you agree. Your manager is nearby and heard it but says nothing.',
 'Silence in the face of dehumanizing speech is participation. Scripture calls us to speak up for those who are treated unjustly. This is not about winning an argument — it is about refusing to let hatred pass as normal.',
 '[{"reference": "Proverbs 31:8-9", "text": "Open your mouth for the mute, for the rights of all who are destitute. Open your mouth, judge righteously, defend the rights of the poor and needy."}]',
 'You do not need to preach. A simple, calm response: "I do not agree with that." Or: "That is not how I see people." Then continue professionally. You have drawn a line without escalating. Your coworker who heard that — who may have been wounded by it — will remember you spoke.',
 'God, give me the courage to say something when saying nothing would be easier. Let my words be firm without being cruel, clear without being self-righteous.',
 'When you have heard prejudiced speech on the floor, what did you do? What do you wish you had done?',
 true),

-- Customer 5
('customer-wont-take-no',
 'customer',
 'The Customer Who Will Not Accept No',
 'You have explained the numbers clearly three times. The payment is what it is. They keep pushing for something that does not exist — a lower rate, a bigger discount, a payment that the math simply will not support. They are not angry, just relentless.',
 'There is a difference between serving someone and being worn down by them. Even Jesus, who healed and served endlessly, sometimes withdrew from the crowd. Setting a boundary is not a failure of service.',
 '[{"reference": "Luke 5:16", "text": "But he would withdraw to desolate places and pray."}]',
 'Repeat your best offer clearly one final time. "This is the best I can do today. I wish I had a different answer. If anything changes, I will call you immediately." Then stop negotiating. You have done your job.',
 'Lord, give me wisdom to know when persistence is service and when it is enabling. Help me let go of deals I cannot close without losing my peace.',
 'Do you find it hard to walk away from a deal that is going nowhere? What are you afraid of losing — the commission, the manager''s approval, or something else?',
 false),

-- Customer 6
('customer-prayed-for-deal',
 'customer',
 'The Customer Who Says "God Told Me to Buy This Car"',
 'A customer invokes God''s will in the negotiation. "I have been praying about this vehicle." They use spiritual language to pressure you on price, as if you are interfering with divine providence by not discounting further.',
 'God is not a negotiation tactic. Using his name to manipulate a transaction trivializes faith and tests the sincerity of everyone involved. Scripture warns against using the Lord''s name carelessly.',
 '[{"reference": "Exodus 20:7", "text": "You shall not take the name of the Lord your God in vain, for the Lord will not hold him guiltless who takes his name in vain."}]',
 'Respond calmly without challenging their sincerity: "I respect that you are praying about this decision. Here is what I can do on the price — and I trust God will guide you whether that works for your budget or not." You honor their stated faith without letting it manipulate you.',
 'Father, keep me from using your name to close a deal, and give me grace when others do. Let my yes be yes and my no be no.',
 'Have you ever invoked God or faith in a sales conversation in a way that, looking back, was manipulative rather than sincere?',
 false),

-- Customer 7 (premium)
('customer-child-in-tow',
 'customer',
 'The Customer with a Scared Child in Tow',
 'A parent is test-driving with a young child who is clearly anxious — crying, clinging, overwhelmed. The parent is frazzled, trying to focus on the car and the numbers while managing a meltdown. They look like they are about to cry too.',
 'Compassion interrupts the sales process and that is okay. Jesus welcomed children when his disciples tried to send them away. Seeing the whole person — including the stressed parent — is more important than the flow of the deal.',
 '[{"reference": "Mark 10:14", "text": "Let the children come to me; do not hinder them, for to such belongs the kingdom of God."}]',
 'Pause the process. Offer a quiet space, a bottle of water, a coloring page if you have one. Say: "Take your time. There is no rush. The car will still be here." You may lose the deal today, but that parent will remember your kindness for years.',
 'Jesus, help me see the whole person in front of me — not just the buyer, but the parent, the spouse, the human being with a life that is hard some days.',
 'When was the last time you put a customer''s wellbeing ahead of the deal and felt good about it afterward?',
 true),

-- Customer 8
('customer-spouse-conflict',
 'customer',
 'The Couple Fighting in Your Showroom',
 'A married couple is test-driving a vehicle and they cannot agree on anything — budget, color, features. Voices are rising. One of them snaps at the other. You are standing there, caught in the crossfire, unsure whether to intervene or disappear.',
 'Peacemaking is a kingdom calling, even in a showroom. But you are not their counselor. Your role is to stay calm, stay neutral, and not take sides in a conflict you did not create.',
 '[{"reference": "Matthew 5:9", "text": "Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God."}]',
 'Step back physically. Say: "This is a big decision. Would it help if I gave you two some space to talk privately? I can be outside when you are ready." You remove yourself from the triangle and give them room to resolve things.',
 'Lord, when I am caught in other people''s conflict, help me to be a calming presence rather than a complicating one. Let me not exploit tension to push a deal.',
 'How do you handle it when customers argue in front of you? Do you stay quiet, try to mediate, or use it to steer the deal?',
 false),

-- Customer 9
('customer-dream-car-cant-afford',
 'customer',
 'The Customer Stretching for a Dream Car They Cannot Afford',
 'A young buyer has fallen in love with a vehicle that is twice their realistic budget. They are approved for it — barely — at a term and rate that will bury them. They are excited. They are signing. You know this is going to hurt them.',
 'Love for a neighbor sometimes looks like protecting them from their own enthusiasm. The writer of Proverbs warned against surety — putting up what you cannot afford to lose. You are not responsible for their choices, but you are responsible for what you recommend.',
 '[{"reference": "Proverbs 22:26-27", "text": "Be not one of those who give pledges, who put up security for debts. If you have nothing with which to pay, why should your bed be taken from under you?"}]',
 'Pause before the paperwork. Say: "I want to show you another option that gives you more breathing room each month. If you still want this one after seeing both, that is your call — but I want you to go home with a payment that lets you sleep at night."',
 'Father, let me not be complicit in someone else''s financial harm, even if they are asking for it. Give me the courage to show restraint when the sale is right in front of me.',
 'Have you ever sold someone a car you knew they could not afford? How do you feel about it now?',
 false),

-- Customer 10 (premium)
('customer-terminal-illness',
 'customer',
 'The Customer Buying a Car Before They Die',
 'A customer discloses quietly — almost as an aside — that they have a terminal diagnosis. They want a nice vehicle for the time they have left. Maybe it is a bucket-list car. Maybe they just want something reliable for doctor visits. The disclosure hangs in the air. You do not know what to say.',
 'This is holy ground. Do not rush to fill the silence. Job''s friends did their best work in the first seven days — when they said nothing and just sat with him. Your presence, not your pitch, is what is needed here.',
 '[{"reference": "Job 2:13", "text": "And they sat with him on the ground seven days and seven nights, and no one spoke a word to him, for they saw that his suffering was very great."}]',
 'Slow everything down. Let them talk. Ask gentle questions: "What kind of driving do you want to do in it?" Not "What payment works for you?" Help them find the right vehicle, not the most expensive one. If you pray, offer to pray with them — but only if it feels natural, not scripted.',
 'Jesus, when I encounter someone facing death, help me to be fully present. Let me not treat them as a transaction. Let me be a human being first, a salesperson second.',
 'What do you fear about conversations that touch on death and mortality? What would help you be present rather than retreat?',
 true),

-- Customer 11
('customer-returns-for-no-reason',
 'customer',
 'The Customer Who Wants to Return a Car for No Reason',
 'A customer bought a vehicle last week. Now they are back, unhappy — not because anything is wrong with the car, but because they have buyer''s remorse, or their relative disapproved, or they just changed their mind. They want you to "make it right" for something that is not your fault.',
 'Doing the right thing does not always mean giving customers everything they ask for. It means treating them fairly while being honest about what is reasonable. Solomon prayed for wisdom to discern justice — the ability to know what is truly right in a messy situation.',
 '[{"reference": "1 Kings 3:9", "text": "Give your servant therefore an understanding mind to govern your people, that I may discern between good and evil."}]',
 'Listen fully before responding. Acknowledge their feelings: "I understand this is not what you expected." Then explain clearly what is possible within your dealership''s policies. If there is a fair compromise, offer it. If not, say so kindly. You cannot undo every decision someone regrets.',
 'Lord, give me the discernment to know when to bend and when to hold firm. Let me treat disappointed customers with dignity without accepting blame that is not mine.',
 'When a customer blames you for their own decision, what rises up in you — defensiveness, guilt, or something else?',
 false),

-- Customer 12
('customer-gift-for-struggling-child',
 'customer',
 'The Parent Buying a Car for a Struggling Adult Child',
 'A parent is buying a vehicle for their adult child who has been through rehab, jail, or a divorce. They are spending money they do not really have because they hope a reliable car will help their child get back on their feet. They are anxious, hopeful, and trying not to cry.',
 'This parent is imaging God''s prodigal love — reckless, hopeful, willing to spend on someone who may not deserve it. Your job is to help them make a wise purchase, not to exploit their hope.',
 '[{"reference": "Luke 15:20", "text": "But while he was still a long way off, his father saw him and felt compassion, and ran and embraced him and kissed him."}]',
 'Ask about the child — not to pry, but to understand. "What does your son need this car to do for him?" Help them find something safe, reliable, and affordable. Do not upsell. This is not the deal to maximize gross.',
 'Father, when I see someone spending love on a child who has struggled, help me to honor that love with honesty and restraint. Let me be part of a story of restoration, not exploitation.',
 'How does your family background shape the way you see customers who are parents making sacrifices for their children?',
 false),

-- ─── MANAGEMENT (12 situations, 4 premium) ────────────────────────────────────

-- Management 1
('mgmt-pad-the-payment',
 'management',
 'Manager Wants You to Pad the Payment',
 'Your sales manager tells you to quote a payment thirty dollars higher than the actual number so there is "room to negotiate." "Everyone does it," they say. "The customer expects it." You know that thirty dollars matters to this customer, who has already told you their budget is tight.',
 'Deception is not a tool of the kingdom, even when it is industry standard. A false balance is a false balance, whether it is a pound of grain or a car payment. Integrity may cost you favor with your manager, but it will not cost you your soul.',
 '[{"reference": "Proverbs 20:10", "text": "Unequal weights and unequal measures are both alike an abomination to the Lord."}]',
 'Respond respectfully but clearly: "I understand the tactic, but I would rather give them the real number and negotiate honestly from there." If the manager insists, ask: "Can we try it my way on this one deal? If I cannot close it honestly, I will follow your lead next time."',
 'Lord, give me the courage to push back against practices I know are dishonest, even when the person pushing is my boss. Let my integrity be more important than my commission.',
 'Have you ever padded a payment because your manager told you to? How did it sit with you afterward?',
 false),

-- Management 2 (premium)
('mgmt-split-deal-unfairly',
 'management',
 'Manager Takes Half Your Deal for Minimal Work',
 'You worked a customer for four hours — test drive, negotiation, finance prep. A manager swoops in for the final ten minutes and marks it a split. When you object, they say "that is how it works here." This is the third time this month.',
 'Justice in the workplace matters to God. The laborer is worthy of their wages. While Scripture calls us to generosity, it does not call us to passive acceptance of exploitation. There is a difference between turning the other cheek and being a doormat.',
 '[{"reference": "1 Timothy 5:18", "text": "For the Scripture says, You shall not muzzle an ox when it treads out the grain, and, The laborer deserves his wages."}]',
 'Document what happened. Have a private, calm conversation with the manager: "I want to understand how splits are determined so I can plan my deals accordingly. On this one, I did the full walk, test drive, and negotiation. I would like to revisit the split." If the pattern continues, consider whether this is the right store for you.',
 'Father, give me wisdom to advocate for myself without becoming bitter. If this store is not where I am supposed to be, give me clarity and courage to move.',
 'What is the difference between being a team player and being taken advantage of? Where is that line for you?',
 true),

-- Management 3
('mgmt-pressure-to-close-sunday',
 'management',
 'Pressure to Work Sundays',
 'Your store is open Sundays, and your manager has made it clear that "the serious salespeople work Sundays." You have been trying to keep Sunday for worship and family rest, but every Sunday you miss, your name slides down the board and your manager''s attitude toward you cools.',
 'The Sabbath is a gift, not a burden. God rested — not because he was tired, but to establish a rhythm of work and rest for his people. Choosing rest in a business that punishes it is an act of trust that God, not your hours, is your provider.',
 '[{"reference": "Exodus 20:8-10", "text": "Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy. Six days you shall labor, and do all your work, but the seventh day is a Sabbath to the Lord your God."}]',
 'Be clear with your manager: "I work hard Monday through Saturday. Sunday is for my faith and my family. I understand that affects my numbers, and I accept that trade-off." Then let your performance on the other six days speak. A manager who sees your numbers will often stop pushing.',
 'Lord, help me trust that you provide for me, not my hours on the floor. Give me the courage to honor the Sabbath even when it costs me.',
 'What would it look like to truly rest one day a week? What would you lose — and what might you gain?',
 false),

-- Management 4
('mgmt-withheld-spiff',
 'management',
 'Manager Withheld a Spiff You Earned',
 'There was a spiff on a specific model — 500 dollars extra per unit. You sold three last month. The spiff never appeared on your commission sheet. When you asked, the manager said the promotion had "fine print" that excluded your deals. You suspect this is not true.',
 'God cares about honest compensation. James thunders against employers who withhold wages. You do not have to be combative to be clear. Ask for what you earned, and trust God with the outcome.',
 '[{"reference": "James 5:4", "text": "Behold, the wages of the laborers who mowed your fields, which you kept back by fraud, are crying out against you."}]',
 'Ask to see the written spiff terms. If they were not provided, escalate calmly to the general manager. Phrase it as a question, not an accusation: "I want to understand the terms so I know how to plan my deals going forward."',
 'God, you see what is taken from me. Help me to advocate for fair treatment without letting money become the thing I am living for.',
 'How do you handle it when you suspect you are being shorted on pay? Do you speak up or stay quiet?',
 false),

-- Management 5 (premium)
('mgmt-tells-you-to-lie',
 'management',
 'Manager Tells You to Lie About Vehicle History',
 'A used car has a minor accident on the Carfax. The manager tells you that if the customer does not ask, you do not have to bring it up. "It was just a fender bender. The car is fine." But you know the customer is trusting you to be honest about what they are buying.',
 'Omitting the truth when disclosure is expected is still deception. The Ninth Commandment is not just about outright lies — it is about bearing faithful witness. Your word is the only thing you take with you when you leave this dealership.',
 '[{"reference": "Exodus 20:16", "text": "You shall not bear false witness against your neighbor."}]',
 'Say: "I want to be upfront with every customer about a vehicle''s history. If there is nothing to hide, there is no reason not to disclose." If the manager punishes you for that, you have learned something important about where you work.',
 'Lord, let my yes be yes and my no be no. When my manager asks me to shade the truth, give me the words to say no respectfully and the courage to accept the consequences.',
 'Have you ever withheld a vehicle''s history from a customer because your manager told you to? How did you justify it at the time?',
 true),

-- Management 6
('mgmt-favoritism',
 'management',
 'Favoritism — The Manager''s Favorite Gets All the Fresh Ups',
 'There is a salesperson on your floor who gets every fresh up, every good lead, every house deal. They are the manager''s golf buddy, drinking partner, or relative. You are working twice as hard for half the opportunities, and everyone knows it.',
 'Favoritism is a failure of leadership, not a reflection of your worth. Joseph was sold into slavery by his own brothers — the ultimate act of favoritism''s dark side — and yet God was with him in the pit, in Potiphar''s house, and in prison. Your circumstance is not your ceiling.',
 '[{"reference": "Genesis 39:2", "text": "The Lord was with Joseph, and he became a successful man."}]',
 'Control what you can control: your follow-up, your product knowledge, your attitude. Customers notice consistency. Over time, referral business from customers you treated well can outpace the fresh-up advantage. If the inequity is severe, have a private, professional conversation with the GM.',
 'Lord, when I see others get opportunities I deserve, keep me from bitterness. Help me to work with excellence in the circumstances I have, trusting that you see me and will provide.',
 'How does it affect your work when you feel like the playing field is not level? What helps you stay focused on what you can control?',
 false),

-- Management 7
('mgmt-quota-after-bad-month',
 'management',
 'The Quota Conversation After a Bad Month',
 'It is the last week of the month and you are three units short of quota. Your manager calls you into the office. The tone is not encouraging — it is threatening. "If you do not hit your number, we need to have a different conversation." You feel your stomach drop.',
 'Your worth is not measured in units. The prodigal son''s father did not love him based on his performance — he ran to him while he was still far off, still a failure by every visible measure. God''s love is not quota-based, and your identity is held in that love, not on the whiteboard.',
 '[{"reference": "Luke 15:20", "text": "But while he was still a long way off, his father saw him and felt compassion, and ran and embraced him and kissed him."}]',
 'Breathe. This conversation does not define you. Respond calmly: "I understand the expectation. Here is my plan for the rest of the month." Then work the plan. Whether you hit quota or not, you are still a child of God, still called, still loved.',
 'Father, when my performance is measured and found wanting, anchor me in the truth that my worth is not on the board. Help me to care about my work without letting my work define my value.',
 'When was the last time you faced a quota conversation that made you feel worthless? What truth did you need to hold onto?',
 false),

-- Management 8 (premium)
('mgmt-asks-you-to-rollback-odometer',
 'management',
 'Manager Asks You to Misrepresent Mileage',
 'A trade-in has unusually low miles for its age. The odometer works, but something feels off — maybe a previous rollback, maybe a cluster swap. The manager says to list it "as-is with the miles shown." You are not comfortable, but challenging this could put your job at risk.',
 'Fraud is fraud, even when someone else signs off on it. Ananias and Sapphira were not struck down for holding money back — they were struck down for pretending otherwise. The deception itself was the sin.',
 '[{"reference": "Acts 5:3-4", "text": "Peter said, Ananias, why has Satan filled your heart to lie to the Holy Spirit? You have not lied to man but to God."}]',
 'Do not write the listing. Say: "I am not comfortable listing this without verifying the mileage. If we are going to sell it this way, I would rather not be the one to put my name on it." If the dealership retaliates, this is not a place you want to build a career.',
 'God, give me the courage to refuse what I know is wrong, even when saying no could cost me my job. I trust you with my provision.',
 'Where is the line between "just doing my job" and being complicit in something dishonest? Have you ever crossed it?',
 true),

-- Management 9
('mgmt-berates-you-publicly',
 'management',
 'Manager Berates You in Front of the Whole Floor',
 'You made a mistake — missed a follow-up, fumbled a turn, lost a deal. Instead of pulling you aside, your manager dresses you down in the middle of the showroom, loud enough for everyone to hear. Your coworkers look away. You want to disappear.',
 'Public humiliation is not leadership. It is abuse. Even when correction is warranted, dignity should be preserved. Jesus'' model of correction was often private first: "go and tell him his fault, between you and him alone."',
 '[{"reference": "Matthew 18:15", "text": "If your brother sins against you, go and tell him his fault, between you and him alone."}]',
 'Do not respond in the moment — you cannot win a public fight with a manager. Later, when you are calm, ask for a private word: "I want to improve, and I take feedback seriously. In the future, I would hear it better if it were given privately." This is professional, direct, and sets a boundary.',
 'Lord, when I am humiliated publicly, keep me from shame-spiraling or lashing out. Give me the composure to respond with dignity and the wisdom to know whether this environment is healthy for me.',
 'How do you recover after being publicly embarrassed at work? What helps you get back on the floor?',
 false),

-- Management 10
('mgmt-tells-you-to-skip-compliance',
 'management',
 'Manager Tells You to Skip Compliance Training',
 'The online compliance module is due this week. Your manager tells you to click through it as fast as you can — "just pass the quiz, nobody reads that stuff." But the module covers predatory lending practices and fair credit reporting. You think you should actually read it.',
 'Obeying the law is not optional for followers of Jesus. Paul instructs believers to submit to governing authorities. Compliance training exists to protect customers — many of whom are vulnerable — and skipping it because it is inconvenient is a failure to love your neighbor.',
 '[{"reference": "Romans 13:1", "text": "Let every person be subject to the governing authorities. For there is no authority except from God, and those that exist have been instituted by God."}]',
 'Do the training properly. It is your license, your reputation, and your integrity on the line — not your manager''s. If they push back, say: "I want to make sure I understand the rules so I do not make a mistake that costs the dealership." Frame it as protecting the store.',
 'Father, help me to take seriously the rules that protect vulnerable people, even when doing so is inconvenient or unpopular on the floor.',
 'Have you ever skipped or rushed through compliance training? What was your justification, and does it hold up?',
 false),

-- Management 11 (premium)
('mgmt-kill-deal-for-minor-gap',
 'management',
 'Finance Director Kills Your Deal Over a Tiny Gap',
 'You have a customer committed. They are happy. The deal is clean. Then the finance director rejects it because of a forty-dollar gap in the payment structure that could have been absorbed or restructured. Now the customer is walking, and you have lost a full day''s work and a commission.',
 'Frustration is natural. Rage is a choice. The finance director may be incompetent, rigid, or just having a bad day. Your response in that moment — the words you say, the look on your face, the thoughts you carry home — is yours to steward before God.',
 '[{"reference": "Proverbs 29:11", "text": "A fool gives full vent to his spirit, but a wise man quietly holds it back."}]',
 'Take five minutes before you react. Walk the lot. Breathe. Then, if appropriate, ask the finance director calmly: "Help me understand what happened so I can structure the next deal differently." This is professional, not personal.',
 'Lord, when other people''s decisions cost me money and time, keep me from bitterness. Give me the self-control to respond with professionalism rather than rage.',
 'When a deal dies because of someone else''s decision, how long does the anger stay with you? What does it do to the rest of your day — and who else feels it?',
 true),

-- Management 12
('mgmt-new-gm-changes-everything',
 'management',
 'A New GM Takes Over and Changes Everything',
 'A new general manager arrives. Pay plans change. Expectations shift. Long-standing policies are reversed overnight. The culture you knew — for better or worse — is gone, and you are not sure you want to stay. But leaving means starting over somewhere else.',
 'Change is destabilizing, but it is also a chance to reexamine what you are doing and why. The Israelites spent forty years in the wilderness learning to depend on God rather than Egypt''s predictable rhythms. Uncomfortable transitions can become places of unexpected provision.',
 '[{"reference": "Exodus 16:4", "text": "Then the Lord said to Moses, Behold, I am about to rain bread from heaven for you."}]',
 'Give the new GM sixty days before you decide anything. Watch how they treat people, not just what policies they announce. Meanwhile, quietly update your resume — not as a threat, but as a reminder that you have options. You are not trapped.',
 'God, when everything at work feels unstable, be my steady ground. Give me patience to wait and wisdom to know when change is healthy and when it is time to move on.',
 'What is the difference between "this is hard and I need to push through" and "this is unhealthy and I need to leave"?',
 false),

-- ─── COWORKERS (11 situations, 3 premium) ─────────────────────────────────────

-- Coworker 1
('coworker-skates-your-up',
 'coworker',
 'A Coworker Skates Your Up',
